Given a code and a probability distribution $p$, we can compute the expected code length for the transmission of information about the events. Specifically, the expected length, $\mathbb{E}[\ell_i]$, for each  code $i$ is:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[\ell_1] &= 2\times\frac{1}{2}+2\times\frac{1}{4}+2\times\frac{1}{8}+2\times\frac{1}{8} = 2,\\
    \mathbb{E}[\ell_2] &= 1\times\frac{1}{2}+1\times\frac{1}{4}+2\times\frac{1}{8}+2\times\frac{1}{8} = \frac{5}{4},\\
    \mathbb{E}[\ell_3] &= 1\times\frac{1}{2}+2\times\frac{1}{4}+3\times\frac{1}{8}+3\times\frac{1}{8} = \frac{7}{4}.
\end{align*}
While Code$_1$ may be the most straightforward way to encode these four events, we can find a more optimized code. Although Code$_2$ has a shorter expected code length, it is not decodable. Indeed, the sequence $0111$ can describe both $E_1E_2E_4$ and $E_3E_2E_2$. In contrast, Code$_3$ is decodable and has a shorter expected code length than Code$_1$.

In his seminal paper~\cite{shannon1948mathematical}, Shannon introduced a formula to compute the lower bound for the expected length of a code for a probability distribution. Specifically, given a probability distribution $p$, the Shannon's entropy is defined as
\begin{align*}
    H(p) &= \sum_{i}p_i\log_2 \frac{1}{p_i},
\end{align*} 
with $p_i\log_2 \frac{1}{p_i} = 0$ for $p_i = 0$. %For a given probability distribution $p$, Shannon's Entropy can be viewed as the expected value of $\log_2 \frac{1}{p_i}$, which we call the \textit{surprisal} of the event $E_i$. More likely events have lower suprisal and vice versa.\HW{As I mentioned before, let's not use surpsisal for this, it fits relatvie entropy better.} The suprisal of an event is also the optimal code length for an event.\HW{Is this really true without any extra assumptions? Or do you just mean the lower bound?} Thus the formula for entropy assigns longer codes to less likely events. \tuyen{I tried to tie this into the `assignment' verbiage when introducing cross entropy.}\hanka{I am not sure where you're going with this sentence... The entropy evaluates the whole probability distribution $p$, it does not `see' the specific coordinate values of the vector, so how could it represent only one coordinate ($\log_2 \frac{1}{p_i}$ as you say), and what would $i$ be in this case? Also, the entropy is a function, maybe you want to say, for a given distribution $p$, the value $H(p)$ can be viewed as ...? } \HW{I agree. Entropy does not assign codes at all, it is completely oblivious to any concrete codes  and just gives the lower bound!}

