It may be tempting to consider the two geometries induced by a Bregman divergence to and from a point, separately. 
However, there is a strong connection between the two, given by the Legendre transformation~\cite{Rockafellar+1970}. 


\myparagraph{Legendre transform.} The \new{Legendre transform} $F^*$ of a function $F$ of Legendre type is defined on a conjugate domain 
\begin{align*}
    \Omega^* = \left\{ \nabla F(x)\mid x\in\Omega \right\}
\end{align*}
as 
\begin{align*}
    F^*(x^*) = \sup_{x\in \Omega}(\langle x,x^*\rangle - F(x)).
\end{align*}

This construction induces a canonical map 
\begin{align*}
    \Omega\to \Omega^*, \qquad x\mapsto x^* = \nabla F(x).
\end{align*}
