In contrast, $H_{SE}($tst$_1\|$trn$_1) = 0.371$ is the largest value in its row. In particular, $H_{SE}(\text{tst}_1\|\text{trn}_1) > H_{SE}(\text{trn}_1\|\text{tst}_1)$, which is the reverse of how we expect the soft predictions from the training data and test data to behave. On the surface, this would indicate that trn$_1$ is a poor predictor of tst$_1$. However, as the models were trained to minimize the $KL$ divergence, and because of the difference between the Euclidean geometry and the Bregman geometry induced by the $KL$ divergence, values from $H_{SE}$ do not carry the information theoretic interpretation relevant for analysis. This shows the importance of analyzing these models using the proposed $KL$--Hausdorff divergence rather than the standard one-sided Hausdorff distance.

We see a similar effect for the computation of $H_{IS}$ and $H_{IS}'$; the geometry induced by the $IS$ divergence is drastically different from the geometry induced by the $KL$ divergence, and thus the computations lack an obvious interpretation in this context. However, for speech and sound data, where the $IS$ divergence is used for comparisons~\cite{itakura1968analysis} we would expect the ${IS}$--Hausdorff divergence to carry a useful interpretation. We therefore provide the computations to demonstrate that our algorithms can handle various Bregman divergences.
%Similarly, we provide proof of concept for the computation of $H_{IS}$ and $H_{IS}'$ from the data sets despite not carrying an obvious interpretation in context. Despite also being Bregman divergences, the geometry induced by the $IS$ divergence is drastically different than the geometry induced by the $KL$ divergence.

%Focusing on the final two columns, we have $H_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_1\|\textrm{trn}_2) < H_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_2\|\textrm{trn}_1)$. Geometrically, this says that we need a smaller primal thickening when we center around $R_2$ instead of $R_1$. As the primal KL Bregman balls contains probability vectors which approximate the center, any probability vector in trn$_1$ will reasonably approximate a probability vector in trn$_2$ with maximum loss of expected bits 2.236b < 2.237b. This agrees with $M_1$ having the higher training accuracy score.

%This is also reflected in the dual case, where $H'_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_2\|\textrm{trn}_1)<H'_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_1\|\textrm{trn}_2)$. As these measurements are built from dual Bregman balls, $H'_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_2\|\textrm{trn}_1)$ tells that for any probability vector in trn$_2$, there is a probability vector in trn$_1$ which will approximate it with 4.033b as the maximum loss of expected bits. In contrast, we would have a larger maximum loss of expected bits if any probability vector in trn$_1$ was approximated by a probability vector in trn$_2$.

%Columns 1 and 2 of Table~\ref{tab:BregmanHausdorff_divs} compare tst$_1$ to trn$_1$. In contrast the previous analysis, we have $H_{KL}(\rm{tst}_1\|\rm{trn}_1) < H_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_1\|\textrm{tst}_1)$ and $H'_{KL}(\textrm{tst}_1\|\textrm{trn}_1)<H'_{KL}(\textrm{trn}_1\|\textrm{tst}_1)$. Thus, trn$_1$ needs a smaller radius for both the primal and dual thickening to contain tst$_1$. Inferring from this, tst$_1$ has more spread than trn$_1$. A possible explanation of this is that trn$_1$ has overfitting the training data so points are clustered at the vertices of $\triangle^{99}$. 

