This paper extends Gr\"atz's partial level valuations to obtain new decision
procedures\co{I'd say semantics} for modal logics $\mKT$, $\mKTB$, $\mKTfor$ and $\mKTBfourfive$ using
Ivlev-Kearns restricted non-deterministic matrices. A model extraction
procedure is proposed.


\section{Semantical motivation}

Ivlev and Kearns independently developed semantical characterizations based on four truth values, which, following Kearns, we denote as $\mathcal{V} = \{ \fnec, \fcont, \tcont, \tnec \}$. 

In this account, the designated truth values are $\mathcal{D} = \{ \tcont, \tnec \}$, and each value has the following intended meanings:

\begin{description}
    \item[$w (\alpha) = \fnec$] means $\alpha$ is impossible ($\Box \neg \alpha$);
    \item[$w (\alpha) = \fcont$] means $\alpha$ is contingently false ($\neg \alpha \land \Diamond \alpha$);
    \item[$w (\alpha) = \tcont$] means $\alpha$ is contingently true ($\alpha \land \Diamond \neg \alpha$);
    \item[$w (\alpha) = \tnec$] means $\alpha$ is necessarily true ($\Box \alpha$).   
\end{description}

\begin{definition}[Language]
	We use language defined over a countable infinite set of atomic formulas
with $\neg, \to, \land, \lor, \Box$, and  $\Diamond$ as primitive
operators.\co{Some are derived connectives (reflected also in the table)}
\end{definition}


The modal systems we examine utilize the same tables for the propositional connectives (see Table~\ref{table:PropCondMatrix}). The central distinction between each nmatrix lies in the definitions of $\Box$ and $\Diamond$, which reflect the modal axioms that define each system. 


