%  Automata, Temporal Logic, Verification
%\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Canonical forms play an important role in computer science.
A typical canonical form is born from an insight about the nature of objects it describes,
leading to the development of efficient algorithms manipulating the objects.
In the context of finite words, the Myhill-Nerode theorem about regular languages brought to life a canonical form of finite automata, and led to efficient automata minimization and learning algorithms.

Omega-regular languages do not possess a simple Myhill-Nerode-like characterization,
which led to the flourishing of canonical forms based on representing a language as a family of automata over finite words~\cite{10.1007/3-540-58027-1_27,MALER199793,ANGLUIN201657,10.1007/978-3-031-45329-8_3}.
These models are based on the insight that an omega-regular language can be characterized by the language of its ultimately-periodic words (those of the form $u v ^\omega$).
As a result, these models operate on finite words of the form $u \cdot v$,
which makes them a perfect fit for automata-learning tasks but ill-suited for classical problems of model checking and synthesis.
An alternative canonical representation that does not assume words to be ultimately periodic, and hence is more conventional, was recently introduced by Ehlers and Schewe in~\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22}.
It is based on the notion of \emph{natural colors}.

Natural colors assign colors to words based solely on an omega-regular language and independently of its representation.
This allows an omega-regular language $L$ to be represented as a unique chain of co-B\"uchi languages $L^1 \supset \ldots \supset L^k$,
where $L^i$ is the set of words whose natural color is greater or equal $i$,
and $k$ is the smallest number of colors in a parity automaton recognizing $L$.
By definition, a word belongs to $L$ if and only if its natural color wrt.\ $L$ is even.
Each co-B\"uchi language $L^i$ can be represented by a canonical (and minimal) transition-based good-for-games co-B\"uchi automaton~\cite{DBLP:journals/lmcs/RadiK22}.
Thus, every omega-regular language $L$ has a unique representation as a chain of such co-B\"uchi automata (\emph{COCOA}) $\mc A^1,\ldots,\mc A^k$.

The two distinguishing features of COCOA ---
(1) the chain representation with clearly defined semantics, and
(2) the minimality and good-for-gameness of each-level automaton ---
can potentially be exploited to design better verification and synthesis algorithms. For instance,
COCOA were recently used to solve a reactive synthesis problem that previous tools were unable to handle~\cite{EK24}.

Verification and synthesis problems are usually defined over system behavior descriptions written in linear temporal logic (LTL).
Therefore, to make COCOA useful to verification and synthesis applications,
one needs a procedure for translating LTL to COCOA.
Currently, the only known way to obtain COCOA from LTL formulas is by going through deterministic parity word (DPW) automata:
LTL $\to$ DPW $\to$ COCOA~\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22}.
Although the second step is relatively simple, the first step represents a challenge.
For instance,
one can first translate the LTL property to a nondeterministic Büchi automaton and then apply Safra's determinization construction~\cite{DBLP:conf/focs/Safra88}.
%It is however known for being challenging to implement efficiently.
However, it is well-known that implementing Safra's construction efficiently is challenging.
Alternatively, one can use the LTL-to-DRW translation of Esparza et al.~\cite{DBLP:journals/sttt/EsparzaKRS22} that aims to generate automata with a more regular structure and is thus more amenable to efficient implementations.
%As an alternative,
%Esparza et al.\ recently introduced an LTL $\to$ DPW translation~\cite{DBLP:journals/sttt/EsparzaKRS22}
%that aims to generate automata with a more regular structure and is thus more amenable to efficient implementations.
Nevertheless, in both cases, the overall translation is involved, and the insight of natural colors in the main challenging step is neglected.
This observation raises the question ``\emph{Is there a direct translation from LTL to COCOA?}''.

The second question tackled in this paper concerns the problem of translating from LTL to DPW.
Modern approaches to reactive synthesis and probabilistic verification often reduce to solving parity games~\cite{renkin.23.fmsd,strix,KNP11}, and hence translate from LTL to DPW as an integral part.
Experience from reactive synthesis~\cite{SyntComp} shows that current approaches to this translation still constitute a bottleneck, indicating a need for further research.
We hence ask: ``\emph{Can natural colors enable a simpler translation LTL$\,{\to}\,$DPW?}''

This paper positively answers both questions.
We describe how using the natural colors of words as a starting point leads to a surprisingly simple
%yet optimal
translation from LTL to DPW and COCOA.
Our translation procedure starts from weak alternating automata,
hence it can also translate formulas in linear dynamic logic (LDL)~\cite{DBLP:conf/ijcai/GiacomoV13,DBLP:journals/iandc/FaymonvilleZ17}.
The procedure uses simple operations on weak alternating automata,
Miyano/Hayashi's breakpoint construction~\cite{MHConstruction}, an
augmented subset construction~\cite{10.1109/LICS.2009.32}, and easy graph algorithms.
The resulting automata are highly structured,
%and maintain a connection to the original weak alternating automaton, and hence to the LTL/LDL formula,
making them amenable to further optimizations.
Although the translation procedure runs in sub-optimal triply-exponential time (vs.\ doubly-exponential optimal),
it constitutes a novel path for translating from LTL to DPW, avoiding the intricate constructions entirely.
%The translation procedure works in asymptotically optimal doubly-exponential time.
We now describe ideas behind the new translations.

\subsubsection*{LTL$\,\to\,$COCOA}
We translate a given LTL or LDL formula into a weak alternating automaton $\mc A$ using standard procedures~\cite{MSS88,DBLP:journals/iandc/FaymonvilleZ17}.
Using the Miyano-Hayashi's breakpoint construction~\cite{MHConstruction},
we remove universal transitions from the dual of the weak alternating automaton $\bar{\mc A}$,
which yields an automaton $\mc G$, called an obligation graph, with language $\overline{L(\mc A)}$.
We then build a machine that tracks the suffix languages of $L(\mc A)$ using simple operations on weak alternating automata,
and use this machine to maximize the set of initial vertices of the obligation graph $\mc G$ while preserving its language.
Finally,
we apply the augmented subset construction~\cite{10.1109/LICS.2009.32},
which is roughly the product $2^{\mc G} \!\times {\mc G}$ of the subset construction of graph $\mc G$ with itself.
This yields a nondeterministic co-B\"uchi automaton $\mc A^1_N$ whose language contains all words from $\overline{L(\mc A)}$ as well as words from $L(\mc A)$ with the natural color larger than $1$ -- that is exactly the language $L^1$ of the first level of the COCOA.
The size of $\mc A^1_N$ is in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$, where $n$ is the number of states in $\mc A$.
Thanks to the special structure of $\mc A^1_N$,
it can be turned into a deterministic co-B\"uchi automaton $\mc A^1_D$ without incurring a blow-up beyond $2^{2^{O(n)}}$,
which is then canonized into a good-for-games automaton $\mc A^1$ using the effective procedure of~\cite{DBLP:journals/lmcs/RadiK22}.
The construction of automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$ for level $\ell>1$ is similar, except that it also requires building a product with the previous-level automaton $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ to ensure $L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N)\supset L(\mc A^\ell_N)$.
Since the number of levels can be exponential in $n$,
determinization of $\mc A^\ell_N$ using the breakpoint construction
yields a deterministic co-B\"uchi automaton $\mc A^\ell_D$ of triply-exponential size,
which is then minimized-canonized into the good-for-games automaton $\mc A^\ell$.

\subsubsection*{LTL$\,\to\,$DPW}
The deterministic co-B\"uchi automata $\mc A^1_D,\ldots,\mc A^k_D$ built as a part of translation LTL $\to$ COCOA can be used to construct the DPW $\mc P$ by taking their product.
The parity color of the combined product transition is the largest $\ell$ such that $\mc A^1_D,\ldots,\mc A^\ell_D$ take accepting transitions.
When a word has the natural color $\ell$ ---
thus the co-B\"uchi automata $\mc A^1_D,\ldots,\mc A^\ell_D$ eventually always take accepting transitions while $\mc A^{\ell+1}$ infinitely often takes a rejecting transition ---
the minimal color visited infinitely often by the run of $\mc P$ is also $\ell$.
The DPW constructed in this way has the language $L(\mc A)$, accepts the words by associating them with their natural color, and
%has an asymptotically optimal size in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$.
has at most triply-exponential size.



