\section{Preliminaries}

Let $\bbN = \{0,1,\ldots\}$ be the set of natural numbers including $0$.
We assume that the reader is familiar with the logic LTL;
the formulas of this logic are built over atomic propositions
using the standard Boolean operators and the temporal operators $\LTLU$ and $\LTLX$ as well as operators that can be derived from these two, such as $\LTLG$ and $\LTLF$. % Note by Ruediger: The old description was a bit problematic as it was incomplete by not mentioning W and R.
For an LTL formula $\varphi$, we denote its set of models by $L(\varphi)$.

\subsection{Weak alternating automata}

A useful automaton model for representing LTL specifications on a more technical level are \emph{weak alternating automata}.
We introduce them in a variant that simplifies the presentation from the next section onwards, but is equivalent to the classical definition.
For a set $X$, let $\PCNF(X)$ denote the set of positive Boolean formulas over $X$ in conjunctive normal form, where the special cases of formulas that are equivalent to $\mathbf{true}$ or $\mathbf{false}$ are not contained. 
% PCNF is used implicitly when defining Acc/Rej strategy types via Clauses.

A \emph{weak alternating Büchi automaton} is a tuple $\mc A = (\Sigma,Q,q_0,\delta,F)$ where
$\Sigma$ is an alphabet,
$Q$ is a finite set of states,
$\delta : Q \times \Sigma \to \PCNF(Q)$ is a transition function,
$q_0$ is an initial state, and
$F \subseteq Q$ is a set of accepting states.
The prefix ``weak'' in the name means that the automaton has a total order $\geq$ on states $Q$
which satisfies:
(i) for every $q,x,q'$ where $q'$ appears in $\delta(q,x)$: $q' \leq q$, and
(ii) states equivalent wrt.\ $\geq$ are either all accepting or all rejecting (not in $F$).

Intuitively, an alternating automaton is weak if in every of its strongly connected component (when ignoring edge labels),
either all states are accepting, or all states are rejecting.

The notation $\mathcal{A}_q$ for $q \in Q$ denotes the automaton having the same structure as $\mathcal{A}$ except that the initial state is $q$. Let furthermore $\mathit{Clauses}$ be the set of clauses that appear in any $\delta(q,x)$ for $(q,x) \in Q \times \Sigma$.

Alternating automata accept or reject words,
which is defined through word-checking games defined next.
There are two players, rejector and acceptor,
who on a given word $w = x_0 x_1 \ldots$ construct a play $q_0 q_1 \ldots \in Q^\omega$ as follows.
The play starts in the initial state $q_0$ of $\mc A$;
suppose at moment $i$ the play is in state $q_i$.
The rejector player picks a disjunctive clause of $\delta(q_i,x_i)$,
the acceptor player picks a state in that clause,
say $q'$,
then $i$ is updated to $i+1$ and $q_i$ is set to $q'$.
The strategies of the rejector have type $\Rej : Q^+ \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathit{Clauses}$
and $\Acc : \mathit{Clauses}^* \times \mathbb{N} \to Q$ for the acceptor.
The players construct a play $q_0 q_1 \ldots$ as described above;
it is denoted $\pi(\Rej,\Acc)$ for given rejector and acceptor strategies.
The play is \emph{won} by the acceptor if eventually it visits only accepting states $F$ of $\mc A$,
otherwise it is won by the rejector.
The word-checking game is \emph{won} by the rejector if it has a strategy $\Rej : Q^+ \to \mathit{Clauses}$ to pick clauses to win against every accceptor strategy $\Acc : \mathit{Clauses}^* \to Q$,
otherwise it is won by the acceptor.
Note that both players know the whole word beforehand, and the second parameter to $\Acc$ and $\Rej$ denotes the current index of the word.
Since the word-checking games are of Borel type,
they are determined, implying that $\exists \Rej . \forall \Acc: \pi(\Rej,\Acc)\models \Rej$ is equivalent to $\forall \Acc. \exists \Rej: \pi(\Rej,\Acc)\models \Rej$.
% Note by Ruediger: Memoryless removed for now because it doesn't make sense if the index is also part of the input to the strategies
%Also, note that since the acceptance condition is of the B\"uchi type,
%it suffices to focus on memoryless strategies,
%$\Rej : Q \to \mathit{Clauses}$ and
%$\Acc : \mathit{Clauses} \to Q$.
%The word is \emph{accepted} by the automaton $\mc A$ if the word-checking game is won by the acceptor, otherwise it is rejected.
%The \emph{language} $L(\mc A)$ is the set of all words that $\mc A$ accepts.

With a weak alternating automaton $\mc A = (\Sigma,Q,q_0,\delta,F)$,
we can associate a directed graph $(Q,\to)$
whose vertices $Q$ are the states of $\mc A$
and $q\to q'$ if and only if
there is $x$ such that $q'$ appears in the formula $\delta(q,x)$.
Then, \emph{an SCC (strongly connected component) of $\mc A$} is an SCC of the associated graph $(Q,\to)$.
Note that we will always reason about maximal SCCs in this paper, i.e., SCCs that cannot be extended by a state or vertex without losing the property that it is indeed an SCC.
When the automaton is weak,
every maximal SCC of $(Q,\to)$ either consists entirely of accepting states or rejecting states.

Figure~\ref{fig:awa-example} shows a weak alternating automaton that accepts exactly all words satisfying the LTL formula $\LTLFG a \lor \LTLGF b$.
The left part of the automaton checks $\LTLFG a$, the right one checks $\LTLGF b$.
The language $L(\mc A_{f_2}) = \emptyset$,
$\mc A_{f_1}$ characterizes $\LTLG a$,
and $\mc A_{f_2}$ characterizes $\LTLFG a$.
Similarly,
$L(\mc A_{g_2}) = \Sigma^\omega$,
$\mc A_{g_1}$ characterizes $\LTLF b$, and
$\mc A_{g_0}$ characterizes $\LTLGF b$.

