Note that since for notational simplicity, we assume that the transition functions of alternating automata map every combination of state and input to a conjunctive normal form Boolean formula, the representation of the transition function may be of size exponential in the length of the LDL/LTL formula.


\subsection{Chain of co-Büchi automata (COCOA)}

This section defines COCOA:
a canonical representation of omega-regular languages introduced in~\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22}.
We first define co-B\"uchi automata,
state a known result about canonisation of such automata,
then we define a canonical separation of a given language into subsets,
and finally we combine those notions into a canonical representation of omega-regular languages.

\subsubsection{Canonical co-B\"uchi automata}

A \emph{transition-based nondeterministic co-B\"uchi automaton} (NCW)
$\mc A = (\Sigma, Q,q_0,\delta,F)$
consists of an alphabet $\Sigma$,
a set of states $Q$,
an initial state $q_0 \in Q$,
a transition relation $\delta \subseteq Q \x \Sigma \x Q$,
and a set of accepting transitions $F \subseteq \delta$.

A \emph{path} of $\mc A$ is a (finite or infinite) sequence of transitions
$\pi = (q_1,x_1,q_2)(q_2,x_2,q_3)\ldots$ where $(q_i, x_i, q_{i+1}) \in \delta$ for all $i \in \{1,\ldots,|\pi|\}$ (or $i \in \mathbb{N}$ if $\pi$ is infinite).
A \emph{run} of $\mc A$ on an infinite word $x_0 x_1 \ldots$
is an infinite path $(q_0,x_0,q_1)(q_1,x_1,q_2)\ldots$ that starts in the initial state $q_0$;
a word might have several runs or no run at all.
A run is \emph{accepting} if eventually only accepting transitions appear in it.
A word is \emph{accepted} if it has an accepting run.
The \emph{language} of $\mc A$ is the set of words accepted by $\mc A$.

\sloppypar A co-Büchi automaton $\mc A$ is \emph{good-for-games} (GFG) if there exists a function
$\lambda : \Sigma^* \to Q$, called strategy,
such that for every word $x_0 x_1 \ldots$ accepted by $\mc A$,
the sequence $(q_0,x_0,q_1) (q_1,x_1,q_2)\ldots$, where $q_{i+1} = \lambda(x_0 \ldots x_i)$ for all $i$,
is an accepting run of $\mc A$ on the word.

We remind the reader that deterministic and nondeterministic co-B\"uchi automata have the same expressive power,
and so do NCWs and GFG-NCWs.
Size-wise,
GFG-NCWs are exponentially more succinct than DCWs~\cite{kuperberg2015determinisation}.
Moreover, GFG-NCWs have a canonical form;
these automata in the canonical form are also the smallest in the class of GFG-NCWs.
Turning DCW into GFG-NCW in the canonical form can be done in polynomial time~\cite{DBLP:journals/lmcs/RadiK22}.

Finally, a language is \emph{co-B\"uchi} if there is an NCW having this language.

