%\noindent
%\emph{Canonical chain representation (COCOA)}
\subsubsection{Canonical chain representation (COCOA)}
%Abu-Radi and Kupferman introduced a canonical form of GFG co-B\"uchi automata~\cite{DBLP:journals/lmcs/RadiK22},
%and later Ehlers and Schewe introduced a canonical representation of omega-regular languages~\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22}.
We first introduce a few helpful notions, then proceed to the main definition.

Given a language $L$ over $\Sigma$ and a finite prefix $p \in \Sigma^*$,
the \emph{suffix language} of $L$ after reading $p$ is $L^\mathit{suff}(p) = \{ w \in \Sigma^\omega \mid p\cdot w \in L\}$.
The set of all suffix languages of $L$ is denoted $L^\textit{suff}$.
A finite word $u \in \Sigma^*$ \emph{preserves} the suffix language of $L$ wrt.\ $p$
if $L^\mathit{suff}(p \cdot u) = L^\mathit{suff}(p)$.
For an infinite word $w$ and an infinite set $J \subseteq \bbN$ of positions $j_1<j_2<\ldots$,
a word $w'$ is the result of a \emph{suffix-language-preserving injection} into $w$ at $J$
if it is of the form $w' = w_0 ... w_{j_1-1} u_1 w_{j_1} ... w_{j_2-1}u_2w_{j_2}\ldots$
where $u_i$ for every $i$ preserves the suffix language of $w_0 \ldots w_{j_i-1}$ wrt.\ $L$.
Notice that due to $J$ being infinite,
a suffix-language-preserving injection into a word $w \in L$ may result in $w' \not\in L$, and vice versa.
For instance,
consider the language $\sem{\LTLFG a}$ over $\Sigma=\{a,\bar a\}$ and its word $a^\omega$,
then $(a\bar a)^\omega$ is the result of a suffix-language-preserving injection into $a^\omega$ at positions $J = \{1,3,5,\ldots\}$
but $(a\bar a)^\omega \not\in \sem{\LTLFG a}$.
We will write $w' \in \text{SLII}^L_{J,w}$, or simply $\text{SLII}^L_{J,w}\ w'$,
when $w'$ is the result of a suffix-language-invariant injection into $w$ at positions $J$,
and we may omit specifying $L$, $J$, $w$, whenever they are clear from the context.

The \emph{canonical chain-of-co-B\"uchi-automata representation (\hspace{-0.05mm}\textbf{COCOA}\hspace{-0.3mm})} of an omega-regular language $L$
is the sequence of canonical GFG-NCWs $(\mc A^1, \ldots, \mc A^k)$
where each automaton $\mc A^i$ is defined through its language $L^i$:
let $L^0 = \Sigma^\omega$, then
\begin{align*}
  L^{\text{odd } i} = \big\{ w \in L^{i-1} \mid \forall J.\,\exists \text{SLII}^L_{J,w} w'{:~} w' \in \bar{L} \cap L^{i-1} \,\big\},\\
  L^{\text{even } i} = \big\{ w \in L^{i-1} \mid \forall J.\,\exists \text{SLII}^L_{J,w} w'{:~} w' \in L \cap L^{i-1} \,\big\}.
\end{align*}
The \emph{natural color} of $w$ wrt.\ $L$
is the maximal index of an automaton in the chain accepting the word or $0$ if $\mc A^1$ rejects $w$.
Ehlers and Schewe~\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22} showed that
every omega-regular language has a unique COCOA, and
a word $w$ belongs to $L$ if and only if the natural color of $w$ wrt.\ $L$ is even.\label{page:def:cocoa}. The number of colors in the COCOA is the smallest value $k$ such that $L^{k+1}=\emptyset$. Representing the COCOA as a finite sequence, ending with $L^k$, is justified by the fact that by the definition of languages in the COCOA, we have $\emptyset = L^{k+1} = L^{k+2} = \ldots$.

We now explain the definition, then give examples.
First, observe that COCOA elements have shrinking languages: $L^1 \supset \ldots \supset L^k$.
The language $L(\mc A^1)$ contains all words of $\bar L$,
as well as those words $w \in L$ that can be made into $w' \in \bar L$ by the SLII operation for every given $J$.
The language $L(\mc A^2)$ removes from $L(\mc A^1)$ every $\bar L$-word that cannot be made into an $L$-word in $L(\mc A^1)$ by the SLII operation for every given $J$.
Similarly, $L(\mc A^3)$ is the result of removing $L$-words from $L(\mc A^2)$ that cannot be made into an $\bar L$-word by the SLII operation.
And so on.
Thus, COCOA structures the original language into an onion-like manner,
where each $L^{\text{odd } i}$ removes certain words in $L \cap L^{i-1}$ and
each $L^{\text{even } i}$ removes certain words in $\bar L \cap L^{i-1}$.

\parit{Examples.}
Consider the safety language $L(\LTLG a)$ over the alphabet $\Sigma = \{a,\bar a\}$.
Its COCOA consists of a single $\mc A^1$ with $L(\mc A^1) = L(\LTLF \bar a)$:
indeed, for every $w\in L(\mc A^1)$ and suffix indices $J\subseteq \bbN$,
we take the SLII $w' = w$ to exhibit $w' \in \bar L$.
Note that $L(\mc A^1)$ contains no words from $L(\LTLG a)$,
because only injecting subwords of the form $a^*$ would be suffix-language preserving,
and injecting them cannot move the word outside of $L(\LTLG a)$.
Thus, every word $w \in L(\LTLG a)$ has the natural color $0$.
Consider the language $L(\LTLFG a)$.
Its COCOA $(\mc A^1,\mc A^2)$ has $L(\mc A^1) = \Sigma^\omega$ and $L(\mc A^2) = L(\LTLFG a)$, justified as follows.
For any prefix $p$, the suffix language of $L(\LTLFG a)$ after reading $p$ equals $L(\LTLFG a)$,
hence inserting anything preserves the suffix language.
In particular, we can insert $\bar a$, yielding the SLII $w' \in \overline{L(\LTLFG a)}$ regardless of $w$,
thus $L(\mc A^1) = \Sigma^\omega$.
The case of $\mc A^2$ is straightforward.
Consider the language $L(\LTLGF a \to \LTLGF b)$ over $\Sigma = 2^{\{a,b\}}$:
it has the COCOA $(\mc A^1,\mc A^2)$ with
$L(\mc A^1) = L(\LTLFG\bar b)$ and $L(\mc A^2) = L(\LTLFG \bar a \land \LTLFG \bar b)$, justified as follows.
The language $L(\mc A^1)$ must contain every word from $L(\LTLGF a \land \LTLFG \bar b)$ and
all $L$-words that can be turned into an $\bar L$-word by the SLII operation for every given $J$.
It is not hard to see that $L(\mc A^1) = L(\LTLFG \bar b)$.
The language $L(\mc A^2)$ is the result of removing $\bar L$-words from $L(\mc A^1)$
for which there exists $J$ such that no SLII at $J$ results in a word in $L\cap L(\mc A^1)$.
We skip the precise analysis and only state that $L(\mc A^2) = L(\LTLGF a \land \LTLFG \bar b)$.
Finally, the language $L(\LTLGF a \to (\LTLGF b \land \LTLFG c))$ over $\Sigma = 2^{\{a,b,c\}}$ has the COCOA $(\mc A^1,\mc A^2,\mc A^3,\mc A^4)$ with
$L(\mc A^1) = \Sigma^\omega$,
$L(\mc A^2) = L(\LTLFG \bar a \lor \LTLFG c)$,
$L(\mc A^3) = L(\LTLFG c \land \LTLFG \bar b)$, and
$L(\mc A^4) = L(\LTLFG c \land \LTLFG \bar b \land \LTLFG \bar a)$.
In all these examples, we do not define COCOA elements explicitly but rather through their languages.

\begin{fact}[\cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/EhlersS22}]\label{fact:cocoa-nof-levels}
  Every weak alternating automaton $\mc A$ with $n$ states can be translated into the COCOA
  with the number of levels in $2^{O(n)}$ and
  the total number of states in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$,
  in time in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$.
\end{fact}

