\subsection{The suffix-language tracking machine}
\label{sec:SLTM}

We use the following notation:
for a transition relation $\delta^\mc G$ of an obligation graph and a prefix word $p \in \Sigma^*$,
$\delta^\mc G(v,p)$ denotes the vertices in which a run of $\mc G$ can be after reading $p$ when starting in $v$; we write $v_0 \stackrel{p}{\rightsquigarrow} v$ when $v \in \delta^\mc G(v_0,p)$.

Let $L$ be a language over an alphabet $\Sigma$.
The \emph{suffix-language tracking machine (SLTM)} of $L$
is a Moore machine $\mc M = (\Sigma, L^\textit{suff}, Q^S, q^S_0, \delta^S, \lambda)$
with input alphabet $\Sigma$,
output alphabet $L^\textit{suff}$ (the suffix languages of $L$),
transition function $\delta^S : Q^S \x \Sigma \to Q^S$,
which we overload in the standard way to accept finite prefixes in addition to single letters,
and the labelling $\lambda : Q^S \to L^\textit{suff}$ such that
for each prefix $p \in \Sigma^*$:
$\lambda(\delta^S(q_0^S,p)) = L^\textit{suff}(p)$.
%$\lambda(q^S_0) = L$ and
%for each $q \in Q^S$ and $x \in \Sigma$:
%$\lambda(q) \cap \{x \} \cdot \Sigma^\omega = \{ x w \mid w \in \lambda(\delta^S(q,x)) \}$.

We now define SLTMs with a special structure.
A \emph{canonical SLTM} for a weak alternating automaton $\mc A$ is an SLTM
$(\Sigma, L^\textit{suff}, Q^S, q^S_0, \delta^S, \lambda)$
with $Q^S \subseteq 2^V$,
where $V$ are the vertices of the obligation graph $\mc G$ of $\mc A$,
$L^\textit{suff}$ is the set of all suffix-languages of $L(\mc A)$,
and every $q^S \in Q^S$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:def:SLTM}
  q^S = \big\{ v \mid \exists p\in\Sigma^* \textit{such that } L^\textit{suff}(p) = \lambda(q^S) \textit{ and } v_0 \stackrel{p}{\rightsquigarrow} v \big\}.
\end{equation}
Note that applying the subset construction to $\mc G$ does \emph{not} in general yield a canonical SLTM,
which is easy to see, such as by comparing the definition of the inital states in both constructions.

We now prove the main technical lemma about SLTMs.
\begin{lemma} \label{lem:canonical-SLTM}
  A canonical SLTM for a weak alternating automaton $\mc A$ with $n$ states and a transition function representation that is at most exponential in $n$ can be constructed in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$ time.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof]
  Let $\mc G = (\Sigma,V,V_0,\delta^\mc G)$ be the obligation graph
  (i.e., the MH's construction applied to $\mc{\bar A}$)
  with the singleton initial set $V_0 = \{v_0\}$;
  it can be constructed in $2^{O(n)}$ time.
  Given $\mc G$,
  we construct a canonical SLTM by first constructing a naive SLTM and then minimizing it.

  In the following,
  let $\mathsf{Suffix} : 2^{\Sigma^\omega} \!\!\times \Sigma^* \to 2^{\Sigma^\omega}$ be a function mapping a language $L \subseteq \Sigma^\omega$ and a finite word $p \in \Sigma^*$ to $\{ w \in \Sigma^\omega \mid p\cdot w \in L\}$, i.e., the suffix language of $L$ for $p$. 


  \emph{Step 1: naive SLTM.}
  A naive SLTM
  $\mc M = (\Sigma,L^\textit{suff},Q^S,q_0^S, \delta^S, \lambda)$
  is constructed by applying the subset construction to $\mc G$,
  and the labeling function $\lambda$ intersects the suffix languages of individual vertices.
  The initial state is $q_0^S = \{v_0\}$.
  Given a state $q^S \in 2^V$ of $\mc M$ and a letter $x \in \Sigma$,
  the successor state is
  $\delta^S(q^S\!,x) = \bigcup_{v \in q^S} \delta^\mc G(v,x)$.
  All states reachable from the initial state constitute the set of states of $\mc M$.
  The number of states of $\mc M$ is not larger than $2^{|V|}$ hence it is in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$\!\!.
  The label is
  $\lambda(q^S) = \bigcap_{(Q_1,Q_2) \in q^S} \bigcup_{q \in Q_1 \cup Q_2} L(\mc A_q)$.
  Thus,
  the label function ignores the separation of states in the Miyano-Hayashi's construction,
  so we could have used the subset construction instead.
  We now show that the constructed machine satisfies the definition of SLTMs.
  To this end,
  we are going to prove the following single-step condition ($\dagger$):
  $\lambda(q_0^S) = L(\mc A)$, and
  for every state $q^S$ and letter $x$:
  $\lambda(\delta^S(q^S\!\!,x)) = \mathsf{Suffix}(\lambda(q^S),x)$.
  By induction, this condition implies the satisfaction of the definition of SLTMs,
  namely, for every prefix $p$,
  the label $\lambda(\delta^S(q_0^S\!, p)) = \mathsf{Suffix}(L(\mc A), p)$.
