  \emph{Step 2: minimization.}
  Let $\tilde {\mc M} = (\Sigma,L^\textit{suff},\tilde Q^S,\tilde q_0^S, \tilde \delta^S, \tilde \lambda)$ be the naive SLTM constructed in the first step.
  The SLTM $\tilde M$ may violate Equation~\ref{eqn:def:SLTM},
  because a state of the naivse SLTM may miss vertices (for example, the initial state).
  We derive the minimized SLTM $\mc M = (\Sigma, L^\textit{suff}, Q^S, q^S_0, \delta^S, \lambda)$ from $\tilde {\mc M}$ by merging the states with the same suffix language.
  For every suffix language $L'$ of $L$,
  define
  $q^S = \bigcup_{\tilde q^S: \tilde \lambda(\tilde q^S) = L'} \tilde q^S$
  to be a state of $\mc M$,
  i.e.,
  $q^S$ merges all states of $\tilde {\mc M}$ whose label equals $L'$.
  To find out which states should be merged,
  we check whether $\tilde \lambda(\tilde q^S_1) = \tilde \lambda(\tilde q^S_2)$ for every possible pair $(\tilde q^S_1,\tilde q^S_2)$ of states of $\tilde M$.
  Each such comparison can be performed in $2^{O(n)}$ time\footnote{%
    We represent the label $\tilde \lambda$ as a weak alternating B\"uchi automaton
    derived from label's formula.
    Recall that Boolean operations on weak alternating B\"uchi automata
    can be done in polynomial time in the numbers of states of the automata.
    Given two labels $L_1$ and $L_2$ represented by two automata $\mc A_1$ and $\mc A_2$,
    checking whether $L_1 = L_2$ amounts to checking whether the alternating automaton $\mc A_1 \land \neg \mc A_2 \lor \neg \mc A_1 \land \mc A_2$ has the empty language.
    Checking nonemptiness of alternating automata can be done in singly-exponential time,
    for instance,
    by first removing universal transitions using Miyano-Hayashi construction~\cite{MHConstruction},
    then checking nonemptiness of the resulting nondeterministic automaton.
    This gives a singly-exponential cost of the label-equality check. This also holds if the transition relation representation is exponentially bigger than the number of states, as it does not affect the number of states in the nondeterministic automaton.},
    hence the set $Q^S$ of states of $\mc M$ can be computed in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$ time.
    Let us associate with each state $q^S$ of $\mc M$ one arbitrary state $\tilde q^S$ of $\tilde{\mc M}$ that it merged.
    We now define the transition function of $\mc M$.
    Given a state $q^S$ and a letter $x$,
    define $\delta^S(q^S,x)$ to be the (unique) state $q^S_\textit{next} \in Q^S$
    such that $\lambda(q^S_\textit{next}) = \tilde \lambda(\tilde q^S_\textit{next})$,
    where $\tilde q^S_\textit{next} = \tilde \delta^S(\tilde q^S,x)$ and $\tilde q^S$ is the state of $\tilde {\mc M}$ associated with $q^S$.
    The label-equality check can be performed in $2^{O(n)}$ time
    for every given pair of states,
    hence $\delta^S$ can be computed in $2^{2^{O(n)}}$ time.
    The resulting $\mc M$ is indeed a canonical SLTM,
    because we started from the (naive) SLTM,
    the operation of state merging preserves the definition of STLMs,
    and also ensures the satisfaction of Equation~\ref{eqn:def:SLTM}.
