\subsection{Saturated obligation graphs}

Let us consider what happens when the set of initial vertices in obligation graphs is \emph{saturated}.
Let $\mc A$ be a weak alternating automaton,
$\mc G$ its obligation graph with vertices $V$ and initial vertices $V_0 \subseteq V$, and
$\mc M$ its canonical SLTM whose initial state is $q^S_0 \in 2^V$.
\emph{Initial-states-saturated} (or simply \emph{saturated} for brevity) obligation graph is the obligation graph derived from $\mc G$
by setting its initial vertices $V_0 = q^S_0$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:og-initial-states}
  $L(\mc G') = L(\mc G)$,
  where $\mc G'$ is the saturated obligation graph for $\mc G$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  Recall that $q^S_0 = \{ v \mid \exists p: L^\textit{suff}(p) = L(\mc A) \land \exists v_0 \in V_0: v_0 \stackrel{p}{\rightsquigarrow} v \}$.
  Fix a word $w \in L(\mc G')$.
  It has an accepting run $\rho$ starting in $v \in q^S_0$.
  Since $v \in q^S_0$,
  there exists a language-preserving prefix $p$ and $v_0 \in V_0$ such that $v_0 \stackrel{p}{\rightsquigarrow} v$.
  The run $(v_0 \stackrel{p}{\rightsquigarrow} v) \cdot \rho$ is still accepting,
  hence $p \cdot w \in L(\mc G)$,
  and since $p$ preserves the suffix language,
  $w \in L(\mc G)$.
  The other direction is trivial.
\end{proof}

Finally, we are ready to state a crucial lemma,
which says says that in a saturated obligation graph,
the set of reachable vertices on a given prefix characterizes the suffix language.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kind-graphs-subsets}
  For every two prefixes $p_1,p_2 \in \Sigma^*$:
  $$
  L^\textit{suff}(p_1) = L^\textit{suff}(p_2) ~\Leftrightarrow~ \delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_1) = \delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_2),
  $$
  where $\mc G$ is the saturated obligation graph for a given weak alternating automaton with language $L$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
  The left implication holds already for non-saturated obligation graphs.
  Indeed, $L = \overline{L(\mc G)}$,
  so
  $L^\textit{suff}(p)
   = \overline{L(\mc G)^\textit{suff}(p)}
   = \overline{\bigcup_{v\in \delta^\mc G(v_0, p)} L(\mc G_v)}$,
  where $v_0$ is the initial state of the non-saturated obligation graph.
  For $p_1$ and $p_2$, the latter language expressions are equal, hence
  $L^\textit{suff}(p_1)=L^\textit{suff}(p_2)$.
  %%$L^\textit{suff}(p_1) =
  %%  \lambda(\delta^S(q_0^S\!,p_1))
  %%  \stackrel{\textit{by P\ref{SLTM:P4}}}{=}
  %%  \lambda(\delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_1))$.
  %%Similarly,
  %%$L^\textit{suff}(p_2) = \lambda(\delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_2))$.
  %%Since $\delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_1) = \delta^\mc G(V_0^\mc G\!,p_2)$,
  %%we get 
  %%$L^\textit{suff}(p_1) = L^\textit{suff}(p_2)$.

  The direction $\Rightarrow$ follows from properties P\ref{SLTM:P3} and P\ref{SLTM:P4}:
  \begin{equation*}
    \delta^\mc G(V_0^S\!,p_1)
    \stackrel{\textit{by P\ref{SLTM:P4}}}{=}
    \delta^S(q_0^S\!,p_1) \stackrel{\textit{by P\ref{SLTM:P3}}}{=} \delta^S(q_0^S\!,p_2)
    \stackrel{\textit{by P\ref{SLTM:P4}}}{=}
    \delta^\mc G(V_0^S\!,p_2).
  \end{equation*}
\end{proof}

\subsection{Constructing NCW representations of COCOA levels}
\label{subsec:Al}

\ak{todo: change the def of atm: allow for several initial states}

This section describes how to construct the level automaton $\mc A^\ell$ of the COCOA
for the language of a given weak alternating automaton $\mc A$.
We first define a nondeterministic co-Büchi automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$,
then determinize it into $\mc A^\ell_D$, and finally turn it into a canonical GFG-NCW $\mc A^\ell$ representing the $\ell$th COCOA level.

The nondeterministic automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$ will be defined inductively for $\ell \in \{1,\ldots,k\}$,
where $k$ is the number of COCOA levels for $L(\mc A)$.
We do not need to know the number $k$ beforehand:
since all levels have a non-empty language, the number $k$ can be inferred.
Intuitively, the automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$ is the product $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N \x \mc M \x \mc G$
of the previous level $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$, the canonical SLTM $\mc M$, and the saturated obligation graph $\mc G$,
where the SLTM $\mc M$ and graph $\mc G$ are built for $\mc A\,$/$\bar{\mc A}$ for odd/even $\ell$.
The acceptance condition of $\mc A^\ell_N$ inherits the acceptance condition of the previous level $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ and of the graph $\mc G$,
modulo suffix-language-invariant insertions.

We proceed to the definition.
Let $\mc A = (\Sigma,Q,q_0,\delta,F)$ be a weak alternating automaton.
Let $\mc A^0_N$ be the co-B\"uchi single-state automaton with the universal language $\Sigma^\omega$
(it is not a part of the COCOA).
Fix $\ell \in \bbN$, and suppose the automaton $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N = (\Sigma,Q^{\ell-1}\!,Q^{\ell-1}_0\!,\delta^{\ell-1}\!,F^{\ell-1})$ has already been constructed (and its language is not empty).
Let $\mc G = (\Sigma, V,V_0,\delta^\mc G)$ be the saturated obligation graph and
$\mc M = (\Sigma, L^\textit{suff},Q^S\!,q^S_0,\delta^S\!,\lambda)$ be the canonical SLTM,
both for $\mc A\,$/$\bar{\mc A}$ for odd/even $\ell$,
where $L^\textit{suff}$ are the suffix languages of $\mc A\,$/$\bar{\mc A}$ for odd/even $\ell$.
\begin{definition}[nondeterministic $\mc A^\ell_N$]
  \label{def:nondetAl}
  We define the following weak nondeterministic transition-based co-B\"uchi automaton
  $\mc A^\ell_N = (\Sigma,Q^\ell\!,Q^\ell_0,\delta^\ell\!,F^\ell)$:
  \li
  \- The set $Q^\ell\! \subseteq Q^{\ell-1}\! \x Q^S\! \x V$ consists of the states reachable under $\delta^\ell$ from the initial states.
  \- The initial states $Q^\ell_0 = Q^{\ell-1}_0 \x \{ q_0^S\} \x V_0$.

  \- For given $q^{\ell-1}\!,q^S$\!, $v$, and $x$:
    \begin{align*}
      \delta^\ell((q^{\ell-1}\!,q^S\!\!,v),x) &=\\
      \delta^{\ell-1}&(q^{\ell-1}\!,x) \x \{ \delta^S(q^S\!\!,x) \} \x \delta^\mc G(v,x).
    \end{align*}

\- To define accepting transitions,
   we decompose the structure into maximal strongly connected components
   $\{(Q_1^\ell,\delta_1^\ell), \ldots, (Q_n^\ell,\delta_n^\ell) \}$;
   the transient transitions are set to be rejecting.

   Consider one such SCC $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$.
   Since the automaton $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ is weak,
   all states of the SCC $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$ have the $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$-components from the same SCC of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$.
   Let $(Q^{\ell-1}_i, \delta^{\ell-1}_i)$ be the SCC of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ to which all the $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ components in $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$ belong to.
   (Thus,
    when a run of $\mc A^\ell_N$ loops in $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$,
    the simulated run of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$ loops in $(Q^{\ell-1}_i, \delta^{\ell-1}_i)$.)

   The transitions of $\delta_i^\ell$ are either all accepting or all rejecting;  the transitions are accepting if and only if the following two conditions are satisfied.
   \li
   \-[(a1)]\label{Al:acc:a1} The SCC $(Q^{\ell-1}_i\!,\delta^{\ell-1}_i)$ is an accepting SCC of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$.
   \-[(a2)] The set $Q^\ell_i$ contains a state $(q^{\ell-1}\!,q^S\!,v)$ where $v$ is a $\mc G$-accepting state.
   \il
  \il
\end{definition}

The definition is simpler when $\ell=1$:
the transition function becomes 
$\delta^\ell((q^S\!\!,v),x) = \{ \delta^S(q^S\!\!,x) \} \x \delta^\mc G(v,x)$,
and the acceptance definition uses only the condition (a2).

Recall that we defined $\mc G$ depending on $\ell$ in such a way that $L(\mc G) = \overline{L(\mc A)}$ for odd $\ell$ and $L(\mc G) = L(\mc A)$ for even $\ell$.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Ll_includes_AlN}
  For $\ell \in \{1,\ldots,k\}$ where $k$ is the number of COCOA levels for $L(\mc A)$,
  every word accepted by $\mc A^\ell_N$ has a natural color of at least $\ell$:
  \begin{equation*}
    L(\mc A^\ell_N)
    ~\subseteq~
    \big\{w \mid \forall J.\,\exists \textnormal{SLII}^{L(\mc A)}_{J,w} w': w' \in L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N) \cap L(\mc G)\big\}.
  \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  Consider a word $w$ accepted by $\mc A^\ell_N$.
  For a given index set $J$,
  we will build an SLII $w'$ with an accepting run in $\mc G$ and in $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$.

  The original word $w$ has an accepting run $\rho$ in $\mc A^\ell_N$
  that gets trapped in some SCC $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$ of $\mc A^\ell_N$
  consisting solely of accepting transitions.
  Consider an arbitrary state $(q^{\ell-1}_1\!,q^S_1\!,v_1)$ that appears infinitely often in the run when looking only at positions $J$,
  and let $J' \subseteq J$ be those positions.
  By definition of $\mc A^\ell_N$,
  from the state $(q^{\ell-1}_1\!,q^S_1\!,v_1)$,
  %for every accepting SCC of $\mc A$ with states $F'$
  there exists
  a path
  $(q^{\ell-1}_1\!,q^S_1\!,v_1) \trans{x_1} \ldots \trans{x_{m-1}} (q^{\ell-1}_m\!,q^S_m,v_m)$
  in $(Q^\ell_i,\delta^\ell_i)$
  such that the $\mc G$-path
  $v_1 \trans{x_1} \ldots \trans{x_{m-1}} v_m$
  %discharges $F'$.
  visits a $\mc G$-accepting state.
  We now construct an auxiliary finite word $u$ and a finite path $\pi$.
  Consider the following finite word $u = x \cdot y$:
  it starts with the prefix $x = x_1 \ldots x_{m-1}$ mentioned previously,
  followed by a finite suffix $y \in \Sigma^*$ that brings $(q^{\ell-1}_m\!,q^S_m,v_m)$ back to $(q^{\ell-1}_m\!,q^S_1,v_1)$;
  such a suffix $y$ exists since both states belong to the same SCC of $\mc A^\ell_N$.
  Let $\pi$ be the corresponding finite path in $\mc A^\ell_N$ on reading $u$.
  We insert $u$ at positions $J'$ of $w$ and nothing in other positions $J \setminus J'$,
  which yields the word $w'$.
  Note that since the SLTM's state is the same before and after reading $u$,
  each insertion of $u$ preserves the suffix language wrt.\ $L(\mc A)$ for odd $\ell$ and wrt.\ $\overline{L(\mc A)}$ for even $\ell$.
  Preserving the suffix language wrt.\ $\overline{L(\mc A)}$ implies preserving the suffix language wrt.\ $L(\mc A)$.
  Thus, $w' \in \text{SLII}^{L(\mc A)}_{J,w}$.
  By inserting the path $\pi$ into $\rho$ at positions $J'$,
  we get the run $\rho'$ of $\mc A^\ell_N$ on $w'$.
  By our construction,
  the projection of $\rho'$ on the vertices of $\mc G$ yields an accepting run of $\mc G$.
  Finally, by definition of acceptance of $\mc A^\ell_N$,
  the $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$-projection of the original run $\rho$ loops in an accepting SCC of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$, and since the new run $\rho'$ stays within the same SCC of $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$,
  the $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$-projection of $\rho'$ also yields an accepting $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$run,
  and we are done.
\end{proof}

Again, recall that $L(\mc G) = \overline{L(\mc A)}$ when $\ell$ is odd and $L(\mc G) = L(\mc A)$ for even $\ell$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:AlN_includes_Ll}
  For $\ell \in \{1,\ldots,k\}$ where $k$ is the number of COCOA levels for $L(\mc A)$,
  every word of a natural color of at least $\ell$ is accepted by $\mc A^\ell_N$:
  \begin{equation*}
    \big\{w \mid \forall J.\,\exists \textnormal{SLII}^{L(\mc A)}_{J,w} w': w' \in L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N) \cap L(\mc G)\big\}
    ~\subseteq~
    L(\mc A^\ell_N).
  \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  We state four observations, then proceed to the proof.

  A word is \emph{ultimately-periodic} (also called \emph{regular}) if it is of the form $uv^\omega$ for $u,v\in\Sigma^*$.
  Given a language $L$,
  let $L^\mathit{reg}$ denote the set of all ultimately-periodic words of $L$.

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  \emph{Claim 1 \cite{10.1007/3-540-58027-1_27}:}
  For every two omega-regular languages $L_1$ and $L_2$:
  $L_1^\mathit{reg} \subseteq L_2^\mathit{reg} \Rightarrow L_1 \subseteq L_2$.

  %%%%\noindent
  %%%%\emph{Proof.}
  %%%%By contraposition, suppose $L_1 \nsubseteq L_2$,
  %%%%so $L_1 \cap \overline{L_2} \neq \emptyset$.
  %%%%There exists a finite-state automaton characterising this intersection.
  %%%%The automaton has a regular witness of nonemptiness
  %%%%for some regular word from $L_1 \cap \overline{L_2}$,
  %%%%hence $L_1^\mathit{reg} \nsubseteq L_2^\mathit{reg}$.

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  \emph{Claim 2:}
  Suppose for an ultimately-periodic word $w$ and a nondeterministic co-B\"uchi automaton $\mc C$
  the following holds:
  $\forall l\in\bbN.\exists m \in \bbN.\exists \rho$: the run $\rho$ on $w$ visits only accepting transitions between moments $m$ and $m+l$.
  Then the word is accepted by $\mc C$.

  \noindent
  Proof:
  Fix a word $w=uv^\omega$.
  We choose $l = |\delta^\mc C|{\cdot}(|u|+|v|) + 1$,
  where $|\delta^\mc C|$ is the number of transitions of $\mc C$.
  In the provided run $\rho$, between the moments $max(|u|,m) \ldots m+l$,
  some transition will appear twice on the same letter and in the same position relative to the beginning of $v$.
  This can be proven using the standard pigeonhole argument.
  Let $j$ and $k$ be the moments of those two appearances.
  We construct an accepting run of $\mc C$ on $w$
  by mimicking $\rho$ until the moment $j$ and then looping the transitions from $j$ till $k$.

  %In the provided run $\rho$, between the moments $m \ldots m+l$,
  %some transition should appear twice while reading the same letter from the $v$ part of the word.
  %By looping the choices from ... to ...,
  %we construct an accepting run of $\mc C$ on $w$.

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  \emph{Claim 3:} Every word $w \in L(\mc G)\cap L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N)$ is accepted by $\mc A^\ell_N$.

  \noindent
  Proof:
  Fix a word $w\in L(\mc G)\cap L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N)$.
  The word $w$
  together with an accepting run in $\mc G$ and an accepting run $\mc A^{\ell-1}_N$
  uniquely define a run in $\mc A^\ell_N$.
  This run is accepting by definition of acceptance of $\mc A^\ell_N$.

  \smallskip
  \noindent
  \emph{Claim 4:}
  For every two prefixes $p_1$ and $p_2$:\\
  $L^\textit{suff}(p_1) = L^\textit{suff}(p_2) ~\Rightarrow~ \delta^{\mc A^\ell_N}(Q^\ell_0,p_1) = \delta^{\mc A^\ell_N}(Q^\ell_0,p_2)$,\\
  i.e., the sets of states reached after reading $p_1$ and $p_2$ coincide.

  \noindent
  Proof: Assuming the claim holds for $\mc A^{i-1}_N$ (it holds trivially for $\mc A^0_N$),
  we show it holds for $\mc A^i_N$,
  for every $i \in \{1,\ldots,\ell\}$.
  Take a state $(q^{i-1}\!,q^S\!,v) \in \delta^{\mc A^i_N}(Q^i_0,p_1)$.
  By induction hypothesis,
  the automaton $\mc A^{i-1}_N$ after reading $p_2$ can reach $q^{i-1}$.
  The canonical SLTM (built for $L$ or for $\bar L$)
  ends in the same state $q^S$ after reading both prefixes $p_1$ and $p_2$,
  because they have the same suffix languages and
  canonical SLTMs have a single state for each suffix language. %(P\ref{SLTM:P3}).
  Finally,
  the saturated obligation graph after reading $p_2$ can reach the state $v$
  by Lemma~\ref{lem:kind-graphs-subsets}.

  We are now ready to prove the lemma.
  By Claim 1, we focus on regular words only.
  Fix a regular word $w$ such that $\forall J.\exists \textnormal{SLII}^{L(\mc A)}_{J,w} \tilde w: \tilde w \in L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N) \cap L(\mc G)$.
  We show that $w \in L(\mc A^\ell_N)$ by relying on Claim 2.
  Fix a duration $l \in \bbN$.
  Let $J$ be an arbitrary infinite sequence of indices
  separated by $l+1$ steps from each other;
  let $\tilde w$ be an SLII$^{L(\mc A)}_{J,w}$ such that $\tilde w \in L(\mc A^{\ell-1}_N) \cap L(\mc G)$.
  By Claim 3, $\tilde w \in L(\mc A^\ell_N)$.
  Recall that $\tilde w$ is derived from $w$ by inserting some suffix-language-preserving prefixes $u_0,u_1,u_2,\ldots$ at positions $j_0{<}j_1{<}j_2{<}\ldots$ of $J$.
  The automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$ has a run $\tilde \rho$ on $\tilde w$ that gets stuck in an accepting SCC.
  From the run $\tilde \rho$ on $\tilde w$,
  we are going to construct a moment $m$ and a run $\rho$ on $w$ that visits only accepting transitions between moments $m$ and $m+l$ (implying $w \in L(\mc A^\ell_N)$).

  Let $\tilde m$ be a moment when $\tilde\rho$ is in the accepting SCC and is ready to read the original letters of $w$ (not insertions) for $l$ steps;
  let $(\tilde q^{\ell-1}\!, \tilde q^S\!,\tilde v)$ be the state of $\tilde \rho$ at moment $\tilde m$.
  %When the definition of $\tilde q^{\ell-1}$ is unfolded,
  %the state $(\tilde q^{\ell-1}\!, \tilde q^S\!,\tilde v)$ can be re-written as
  %$(\tilde q^S_L,\tilde q^S_{\bar L},\tilde v^1,\ldots,\tilde v^\ell)$,
  %where $\tilde q^S_L$ is the state of the SLTM for $L(\mc A)$ and $\tilde q^S_{\bar L}$ -- of the SLTM for $\overline{L(\mc A)}$,
  %and $\tilde v^i$ is a vertex of the saturated obligation graph of the $i$th-level automaton,
  %for $i \in \{1,\ldots, \ell\}$.

  Let $u_0,\ldots,u_k$ be the suffix-language-preserving insertions performed into $w$
  when reaching the state
  $(\tilde q^{\ell-1}\!, \tilde q^S\!,\tilde v)$.
  Let $\tilde p = \tilde x_0 \ldots \tilde x_{\tilde m-1}$ be the prefix of $\tilde w$ read so far, so the prefix ends right after inserting $u_k$ into $w$.
  Let $p = x_0 \ldots x_{j_k-1}$ be the corresponding prefix of $w$.

  Since $\tilde p$ is derived from $p$ by suffix-language-preserving injections,
  both prefixes have the same suffix language wrt.\ $L(\mc A)$ (and wrt.\ $\overline{L(\mc A)}$).
  By Claim~4,
  the automaton $\mc A^\ell_N$ after reading $p$ can reach every state reached after reading $\tilde p$.
  Therefore,
  there exists a path $\pi$ on reading $p$
  from an initial state into the state $(\tilde q^{\ell-1}\!, \tilde q^S\!,\tilde v)$.

  Finally, we describe the run $\rho$.
  On reading $p$,
  the run $\rho$ follows the above mentioned path $\pi$
  which leads $\rho$ into the same state as $\tilde\rho$ at moment $\tilde m$.
  Then the run $\rho$ mimicks $\tilde\rho$ for $l$ steps,
  so it only visits accepting transitions between moments $m$ and $m+l$,
  and afterwards it proceeds arbitrarily.
  The run $\rho$ and the moment $m=j_k$ satisfy the conditions of Claim 2,
  hence $w \in L(\mc A^\ell_N)$.
\end{proof}

