The most important is the position of the color decoder node within the branch, which determines whether a multi-task network can effectively extract information from different feature spaces. We test 3 structures as shown in Figure \ref{fig_network}, corresponding to the number of sharing blocks of 6, 4, and 2. Our GSsplat uses the middle structure, where color and semantic encoding share the first 4 blocks, each containing 2 convolutional layers. Table \ref{tab4} shows the synthesis results on ScanNet and Replica datasets. It can be observed that an overly shallow or deep shared network will lead to a decline in segmentation performance. In terms of color rendering, the deepening of the encoding network did not bring about obvious enhancement to the image quality results, indicating that colors are more intuitive and easier to learn than semantics. 

