\begin{abstract}
Merging parameter-efficient task experts has recently gained growing attention as a way to build modular architectures that can be rapidly adapted on the fly for specific downstream tasks, without requiring additional fine-tuning. Typically, LoRA serves as the foundational building block of such parameter-efficient modular architectures, leveraging low-rank weight structures to reduce the number of trainable parameters. In this paper, we study the properties of sparse adapters, which train only a subset of weights in the base neural network, as potential building blocks of modular architectures. First, we propose a simple method for training highly effective sparse adapters, which is conceptually simpler than existing methods in the literature and surprisingly outperforms both LoRA and full fine-tuning in our setting. Next, we investigate the merging properties of these sparse adapters by merging adapters for up to 20 natural language processing tasks, thus scaling beyond what is usually studied in the literature. Our findings demonstrate that sparse adapters yield superior in-distribution performance post-merging compared to LoRA or full model merging. Achieving strong held-out performance remains a challenge for all methods considered.



%Our results show that sparse adapters lead to better in-distribution performance after merging w.r.t. LoRA or full model merging, even if delivering strong held-out performance still remains somewhat elusive for all methods.

% {\color{minseon}Multitask fine-tuning has proven effective across various tasks but traditionally requires all datasets upfront, making additional task updates inefficient and often necessitating training from scratch. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods, such as low-rank adapters (LoRA), have been proposed for multitask learning while reducing computational costs and enabling multitask learning by merging multiple LoRAs. However, due to low-rank compression, LoRA may lead to information loss, limiting its ability to match full fine-tuning performance. To overcome this limitation, we propose Sparse-Adapter, a novel parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for large language models (LLMs) that preserves the performance of dense full fine-tuning. By reducing parameter interference, our method enhances generalization on both seen and unseen tasks. Our experiments on 20 seen and 10 unseen FLAN benchmarks demonstrate that Sparse-Adapter outperforms both traditional low-rank adapters (LoRA) and dense fine-tuning approaches. Notably, merging multiple Sparse-Adapters can surpass multi-task learning on unseen tasks, highlighting its potential to significantly enhance generalization in real-world applications.}

%[This is original abstract] Multitask fine-tuning has proven effective in leveraging multiple datasets to improve model performance across various tasks. However, traditional multitask approaches require all datasets to be available upfront, and retraining with new datasets often necessitates starting from scratch. We propose training Sparse-Adapter for large language models (LLMs), a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method that enhances both held-in and held-out generalization. Sparse-Adapter addresses this limitation by providing a practical solution for efficient fine-tuning, designed to be easy to train different experts in parallel and merge as a mixture of experts during evaluation without high parameter interference. \textcolor{red}{Our key contributions are: a) the development of a simple yet effective sparse adapter training method that outperforms dense full fine-tuning, and b) an investigation into how sparse adapters perform when merged.} Our  experiments on 20 held-in and 10 held-out FLAN tasks demonstrate that Sparse-Adapter outperforms both traditional low-rank adapters and dense fine-tuning approaches. Notably, its merged performance on held-out tasks surpasses that of multi-task training, highlighting its potential to significantly enhance generalization in real-world applications.



% We propose Sparse-Adapter, a novel parameter-efficient fine-tuning method for large language models (LLMs) that improves both held-in and held-out generalization. Sparse-Adapter, designed to be easy to train and seamlessly merged for evaluation as a mixture of experts, outperforms traditional low-rank adapters and full fine-tune. We provide extensive study on 20 held-in and 10 held-out FLAN tasks. Notably, we show that its merged performance on held-out data is improved compared to multi-task training, highlighting its potential for enhancing generalization in practical settings.
\end{abstract}

