The following lemma establishes a bijection between natural numbers and tuples of natural numbers whose elements have bounded values, generalizing to tuples a result that was already known for decision trees \cite[Theorem 3.2]{bender2005foundations}. The proof can be found in \cref{se-app:rank-tuple}.

\begin{restatable}{lemma}{leTuple}\label{le:tuple-number}
Let $P$ be the set of all tuples $\langle b_1, b_2, \dots, b_n \rangle$ such that $n \geq 1$, $0 \leq b_i < B_i$ ($i=1 \dots n$), and such that $b_i$ and $B_i$ are natural numbers. There exists a bijective function $\psi$ with domain $P$ and codomain the natural numbers in $[0 \dots \left( \prod_{i=1}^n B_i \right) -1]$. Both $\psi$ and its inverse can be computed in $O(n)$ time.
\end{restatable}


\noindent{\bf Biconnected Graphs.}\label{se:biconnected-graphs}
Let $\mu$ be a node of the SPQR-tree $\mathcal{T}^T$ of a biconnected planar graph $G$ rooted at the edge with minimum identifier. Denote by $d(\mu)$ the depth of $\mu$ in $\mathcal{T}^T$ and by $e(\mu)$ the id of the edge with the minimum identifier in the pertinent graph of $\mu$. We assign to $\mu$ the identifier $\langle d(\mu), e(\mu) \rangle$. We call \emph{conventional order} the order of the nodes of $\mathcal{T}^T$ induced by their identifiers. 

