In the case of interpretation over words one can obtain extra post-composition properties by adding so-called \emph{counting quantifiers}. Let $\FOC$ denote first-order logic extended with quantifiers $\exists^{r{\mod{}} q}x\  \phi(x)$ with the natural semantics that the number of positions $x$ so that $\phi(x)$ holds is congruent to $r$ modulo $q$. Over $\omega$-words, one can even add quantifiers of the kind ``there are finitely many positions" or ``there are countably many positions":
\begin{theorem}\cite[Proposition~3.2]{DBLP:conf/lics/Gradel20}
    \MSO set interpretations over words are effectively closed under post-composition by \FOC interpretations.
\end{theorem}
