\paragraph{Setup.}
We use the LSS and QL implementations of \modes to estimate the maximum and minimum expected accumulated load on seven instances of our model from \Cref{sec:ModellingAndAnalysis}---see \Cref{tab:models} for an overview of their configurations.
%We also use a memoryless ``Uniform'' strategy that resolves nondeterminism by choosing uniformly from all available options.
%
For each instance, besides the uniform run, we execute \modes with the algorithms from \Cref{tab:runs} and let it build confidence intervals of 1\% relative half-width in the final SMC analysis. %---that estimate the min and max loads.
For each model, property, and run configuration, we execute \modes in two modes: the default with fully-observable states (\FO), which considers all model variables observable, and with partially-observable states (\PO) following the \lstinline|observable| annotations in the model.
Each experiment is run until a confidence interval of the requested width is built for the property under study.
We use the center of the intervals to compare the minimum and maximum loads found by each run on each model, keeping track of the wall-clock runtime.
We execute our experiments on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D system (16 cores) with 128 GB of RAM running 64-bit Ubuntu Linux 22.04.

