\section{Preliminary and Related Work}
\textbf{LLM inference phases and workload heterogeneity.} There are two phases within LLM inference: \textit{prefill} and \textit{decoding}. During the prefill phase, the model processes the input prompt to compute the key-value (KV) cache and generates the first token in a single step. In contrast, the decoding phase uses the last generated token and the KV cache as inputs to generate subsequent tokens in a token-by-token manner. Generally, the prefill phase is compute-bound, while the decoding phase is memory-bound~\citep{patel2024splitwise,zhong2024distserve,agrawal2024taming}. 
% \stephan{The memory boundedness is why speculative decoding works. I would try to mention speculative decoding somewhere in the related work as both cascades and speculative decoding are both techniques for speeding up LLM inference. There is also this recent work presented at ICLR: \url{https://openreview.net/forum?id=vo9t20wsmd}. Unless there is a reason you explicitly want to avoid comparing to it I would include it.} 
LLM inference workloads exhibit heterogeneity in input, output token lengths and request arrival rate, which is called \textit{workload heterogeneity}. For instance, conversation workloads (short input and long output lengths) typically require more memory resources to handle the memory-bound decoding phase, while coding workloads (long input and short output lengths) demand more compute resources to manage the compute-bound prefill phase. Therefore, appropriately allocating resources based on workload demands is critical for optimal performance~\citep{zhao2024blendserve,jiang2025thunderserve}.

