\subsection{MILP-Based Inner Optimization}
\label{subsec:ilp}
% The inner optimization of our scheduling algorithm aims to determine the optimal model deployment plan based on the routing strategy and resource constraints. An example of a deployment plan is shown in~\autoref{fig:milp}. Assume we have a total number of $N$ GPUs serving a model cascade with $C$ model types, $\{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_C\}$, with $c_i$ representing the $i$-th model type within the cascade. The outer layer routing strategy provides us with the incoming workload information $\mathcal{W}=\{w_1,w_2,\dots,w_C\}$ for each model type, including details such as the average input and output sequence lengths and the request arrival rate. Additionally, we use $\mathcal{F}=\{f_1,f_2,\dots,f_C\}$ to represent the number of GPUs allocated to each model type. The total number of allocated GPUs must not exceed the total available GPUs, i.e., $\sum_{i=1}^{C}f_i\leq N$.

The inner optimization of our scheduling algorithm determines the optimal model deployment plan based on the routing strategy and resource constraints. An example deployment plan is shown in~\autoref{fig:milp}. Assume a total of $N$ GPUs serve a model cascade with $C$ model types, $\{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_C\}$, where $c_i$ denotes the $i$-th model type. The outer-layer routing strategy provides incoming workload information $\mathcal{W}=\{w_1,w_2,\dots,w_C\}$ for each model type, including average input/output sequence lengths and request arrival rate. We use $\mathcal{F}={f_1,f_2,\dots,f_C}$ to denote the number of GPUs allocated per model. The total allocation must not exceed available GPUs, i.e., $\sum_{i=1}^{C}f_i\leq N$.

