Although there has been considerable research exploring how OCR errors impact downstream NLP tasks in conventional approaches \cite{vanstrien_beelen_ardanuy_hosseini_mcgillivray_colavizza_2020, Hamdi2020}, our results indicate that the performance of mLLMs does not substantially decline with the introduction of OCR noise. Comparing GT TXT-to-CSV and OCR TXT-to-CSV results reveals that across the corpus, the presence of transcription errors only marginally decreases the achieved accuracy rates. Table~\ref{tab:ner-by-variables} highlights performance changes for the different variables and models depending on the input used. For Gemini 2.0 Flash, entity recognition accuracy drops significantly for occupation and address recognition, but remains virtually the same for first and last names, when comparing GT to OCR text input. In contrast, GPT-4o sees a more consistent decline across the different variables measured, when going from GT to OCR text input. 

