the measured delays and the respective corridor, for every recorded value of $T$.
If the delay function is inside the corridor, the contribution to the integral 
is $0$, otherwise it is the distance to the closest corridor border.
By normalizing the resulting value to the integration interval, we obtain 
a value that can be viewed as the average coverage violation, i.e., the
average time deviation from the corridor.

